# The 32-bit words' backend facts as the JVM's int and their conversion lemma, a knowledge
# module an entry imports beside fixed_width_prolog_backend when it holds 32-bit words as
# ints. jvm_int_type is represented natively in the codec jvm_int (com.yang_bo.gezi.jit.JvmIntFamily),
# whose storage is one int (com.yang_bo.gezi.jit.IntStorage); its one constructor is word_type's
# word_cell, of the fields a boolean and the word of 31 bits above it, so a stored int reads
# as word_cell (b ∥ boolean_type) (w ∥ word_type 31) ∥ jvm_int_type. The codec registers no
# native of that constructor: word_cell in the head position is word's, which builds the
# 32-bit word. The thin defines of int32_arithmetic and int32_comparison are bound to the
# natives of com.yang_bo.gezi.jit.JvmIntNatives, which compute them on operands held as ints: the JVM's
# int arithmetic, wrapping modulo 2^32, is word_add, word_subtract, word_multiply and
# word_negate at the width 32, and word_less_than compares two's complement words; a native
# fires only on operands already in the representation, and its result r ∥ jvm_int_type is r
# by theorem (S) of docs/design/gezi-type-preservation-proofs.md. The conversion lemma x ∥ int32_type = x ∥ jvm_int_type is a theorem of the
# β rule (docs/design/gezi-set-equations-proofs.md, section 25), so the e-graph may merge the
# two spellings and the extraction choose one. It is not folded into a backend's preset, so
# that an entry choosing that preset reads as before.
